# Optional portable adapter

The {{ name }} library and CLI work without MCP. The optional stdio adapter exposes
`read_manifest` through the same public library. It does not start when imported and
does not configure a provider or perform model work.

```bash
uv sync --extra mcp
uv run --extra mcp {{ name }}-mcp
```

Configure any MCP client with that executable and arguments. Use an absolute executable
or `uv --directory /path/to/checkout run --extra mcp {{ name }}-mcp` when its working folder
is different. Stdout belongs to MCP; send diagnostics to stderr.
{% if adapter == "mcp-app" %}
The optional MCP App is a read-only manifest viewer. Its Refresh button invokes the
same `read_manifest` tool an agent can call. The view uses the official MCP Apps SDK
and ships as one HTML resource, so consumers do not need Node.js or a CDN.

After editing `ui/src/app.js`, rebuild before packaging:

```bash
npm ci --prefix ui --ignore-scripts
npm run build --prefix ui
uv run --extra mcp pytest
```

Node.js and npm are build-time requirements only. The generated `mcp_app.html` is bundled
inside the Python package with the bundled dependencies' `mcp_app.LICENSE.txt`. The build
collects these notices from dependency packages and fails if a package supplies none.
The view needs no network or device permissions. A host without
MCP Apps can still call the deterministic tool. Opening the view only reads the manifest.
{% endif %}
## Add domain capabilities in the library first

- Add a typed public method, then adapt it for MCP and the optional view. Validate the
  same arguments, authority, and target identities on every path. Keep domain logic out
  of event handlers. UI actions should be available to agents unless policy explicitly
  restricts them; do not use app-only helpers to bypass that restriction.
- For retained work, distinguish work, revision, operation, and view IDs. Use request IDs
  and state preconditions where the library supports them. A receipt is acceptance, not
  completion. Reconcile uncertain work before retrying anything that may spend again.
- Save drafts through explicit library methods with a conflict policy. View context is
  untrusted observation, not durable storage, a user instruction, or approval. Report
  the revision and selection actually shown; do not silently move review to a new revision.
- Separate actor identity, requested effect, and execution grants. A model-call limit is
  not a hard dollar limit. Unsupported limits and unknown usage stay explicit. Never
  expose credentials to the view, and never display missing cost as zero.
- Attaching a view, starting an owned runner, canceling work, and deleting retained data
  are separate effects. Closing a view does not cancel domain work. Reconnect by reading
  retained state, not replaying generation. Forking a conversation does not clone domain
  work; disclose shared attachment or offer a supported clone.
- Keep generated/imported HTML in a separate sandbox without the control view's broker.
  Validate message sources, schemas, and stale grants. Keep resources scoped to their
  work and support remote browsers without credential-bearing localhost URLs.

Use standard MCP tools/resources and MCP Apps metadata, not a host-specific protocol.
The tool owns its domain state. A host owns conversation association and granted host
capabilities. Sampling, Tasks, elicitation, external assets, and device access are optional
negotiated capabilities; this starter does not implement them.

These are development recommendations for optional collaborative capabilities, not new
Smart Tool v1 requirements. Do not add undeclared manifest/descriptor fields or parse
manifest prose as executable setup. The library/CLI and base conformance remain authoritative.

## Validation before claiming support

Run the generated stdio test without provider keys. Add domain fixtures that compare
user and agent results, exercise stale revisions and duplicate request IDs, preserve
drafts across reconnect, and distinguish closing a view from canceling an owned runner.
Test generated-content isolation and denied host capabilities. Check unknown usage and
that each model call is counted once. Finally try the same tool in an independent host,
and an unfamiliar deterministic tool in the host you develop with.

The starter proves manifest transport only. It does not supply domain persistence,
concurrency control, grant enforcement, multi-user editing, or paid-work recovery.

References: [MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest),
[MCP Apps](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps), and
[Python SDK](https://py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/).
